# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them. The =analysis-= notes under =doc/notes= carry the
longer investigations.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* Library

The library itself, =include= and =src=: its behaviour, its interface
and what it carries that it no longer uses.

* Tests

The suite and its driver under =tests=: what it covers, what it
misses, and how it runs.

** TODO The suite against other libraries

The suite tests the C++03 library through clauses 18 to 27. Most of
its assertions check standard behaviour, not the library's additions.
Run against libstdc++ and libc++, a disagreement is a defect in one of
the three or a question the standard leaves open.

Fix: build the driver and the tests of clauses 21 to 27 against
another library. The tests that need the library's additions or its
=_RWSTD_= macros are listed and left out, each with the reason.

Check: the census of the tests that build, a table per library, and
each disagreement read against the standard and filed where it
belongs.

** WAITING ThreadSanitizer cannot follow =longjmp= on glibc 2.44

Under the sanitizer every =longjmp= and =siglongjmp= dies with "can't
find longjmp buf" and exit 66. A ten-line program with =sigsetjmp=
and =siglongjmp= and no signal fails the same way, under GCC 16's
libtsan and Clang 23's runtime alike. The sanitizer records the stack
pointer at =setjmp= and, at =longjmp=, demangles the one saved in the
=jmp_buf= to find the record. glibc 2.44 (a5ec880f80, "Consolidate
pointer guard to use a relro variable instead of the TCB") moved the
x86-64 pointer guard from the thread control block slot at =%fs:0x30=
to the variable =__pointer_chk_guard=; the slot now reads zero, and
the runtimes still demangle with it. The same series gave aarch64 a
rotate its runtime does not undo (78f1f0e39c). Reported upstream as
LLVM issue 228263 (2026-10); GCC's libsanitizer takes the fix at its
next merge from compiler-rt. The six programs of the spot-check note
are the ones this stops.

Fix: the sanitizer runtimes'. The tests need no change.

Check: =0.alloc=, =0.new=, =0.inputiter=, =0.outputiter=,
=18.setjmp= and =18.csetjmp= exit 0 under a sanitizer runtime that
reads the new guard.

* Certification

The scripts under =bin= and the nightly run: building the
configurations, running their suites and comparing the tables with
their pins.

* Locales

The locale sources and charmaps under =etc/nls=, the =localedef= that
compiles them and the databases the facets read.

** TODO The locale sources under =etc/nls= are glibc 2.2's

The definitions and charmaps under =etc/nls= are copies from GNU libc
2.2, as the original =README= says, last changed in 312ef830 (2007).
A survey on 2026-10-13 compared them with glibc's =localedata= at
60a2ae4098 (2.44.9000, 2026-09-29). Coverage: 179 files against 372,
163 language and territory definitions against 351. 139 names match,
20 more once the =.euro= suffix maps to =@euro=; four names are
legacy and ours only (=iw_IL=, =no_NO=, =sr_YU=, =sr_YU.cyrillic=);
192 are glibc's only. Among the 139 shared definitions, after
resolving =copy= directives: 46 currencies differ (=ro_RO= ROL to
RON, =fr_FR= FRF to EUR, =tr_TR= TRL to TRY), 75 currency symbols,
54 thousands separators, 8 decimal points, 45 groupings; =d_fmt=
differs in 36, the month names in 45, the weekday names in 24;
=ja_JP= has no Reiwa era; =ro_RO= collates circumflex before breve
where glibc now does the opposite. The shared tables are older still:
=i18n= says Unicode 3.0, the UTF-8 charmap's widths Unicode 3.2,
against Unicode 14.0.0 in glibc's =i18n_ctype=; =U+1C90=, =U+1E900=
and =U+1F600= are absent from the charmap. =localedef= cannot read
the current sources: glibc's =en_US= with its UTF-8 charmap exits 4,
Error 312, symbolic name =<S02D0>= not found at =iso14651_t1_common=
line 63100, and the keyword table in =util/scanner.cpp= lacks
=alt_mon=, =ab_alt_mon=, =class=, =map= and the conditionals. The
survey compared source values and ran no collation or formatting
oracle, so the counts are differences, not proven defects. The GNU
categories outside the six of C++ (paper, address, measurement) are
not a gap. =doc/notes/analysis-locale-sources.md= holds the survey. The facets can also read the C library's own locales
(=doc/notes/ref-locale.md=), so stale sources need not reach every
=std::locale= a program constructs.

The compiler: =localedef= reads the collation templates: the
=<A>..<B>= range of =collating-symbol=, the conditionals =define=,
=undef=, =ifdef=, =ifndef=, =else= and =endif=, the entries an
ellipsis leads and the ellipsis as a weight. It keeps the bare
collating symbols and the definitions =reorder-after= places after one
of them in one sequence, as glibc does; a name nothing declares there
is a new symbol. A character written raw, in UTF-8, in a collation
statement or in a string reads as its symbolic name, as glibc reads
it. The wide strings took each byte of such a character for a
character, so the wide month and day names, currency symbols and
separators of 431 definitions were wrong (=zh_TW=, =ja_JP=, =ru_RU=,
=fr_FR=, the euro sign of =de_DE=); the narrow strings took the UTF-8
bytes, wrong in the 153 definitions whose charmap is not UTF-8
(=ja_JP.EUC-JP=, =ru_RU.KOI8-R=, =el_GR=). A transliteration statement
that writes its character raw is still skipped (=de_DE=,
=translit_emojis=), and the runtime reads no transliteration at all
(the path in =src/wcodecvt.cpp= is disabled), so what the compiler
writes there goes unread until that is decided. Every one of the 502 definitions in glibc's
=SUPPORTED= builds, each in under 30 s among ten in parallel;
=cmn_TW=, which took nine minutes alone on its stroke template, takes
22 s, and =zh_CN= 20 s on the pinyin one, against 200 s before:
=reorder-after= removed each entry of a block from its old place by a
search of its own. Before the one sequence, 174 definitions built
wrong: a =reorder-after= block that opens with a bare symbol lost the
weights of its definitions, so =es_ES= put ñ and =sv_SE= å, ä and ö
among the undefined characters. An escaped character in a string
stands for itself, as glibc reads it; the GNU sources escape the
slash, so the LC_TIME strings of 262 definitions held doubled slashes
(=en_US= wrote "%m//%d//%Y" for =%x=), the dates of every =era= were
misread, and =es_PE= wrote "S//" for its sol. A date of an =era= that
does not parse leaves bytes of the era record nothing wrote
(=Def::parse_era=, =Def::write_time= under valgrind), and the year of
the Buddhist era of =th_TH= comes out one above the C library's. A
statement led by a keyword no facet reads is skipped by a named table
in =util/def.cpp=: =alt_mon=, =ab_alt_mon=, =date_fmt=, =week=,
=first_weekday=, =first_workday=, =cal_direction=, =timezone= and
=era_year= in =LC_TIME=; =yesstr= and =nostr=; =class=, =map=,
=charclass=, =charconv= and the names those two declare, =outdigit=,
=alnum=, =default_missing= and =translit_ignore= in =LC_CTYPE=;
=symbol-equivalence=, used only by the =LC_COLLATE= of =i18n=, which
no definition copies, =coll_weight_max= and =section-symbol=; the
second currency of =LC_MONETARY=. The six categories of the GNU
sources no facet reads are skipped whole. Any other keyword draws
Warning 713; none of the 502 definitions draws one.
=codepoint_collation= orders every character by its value, as glibc's
C locales do; =C.UTF-8= tied every character before. Its collation
database is 7 MB, one weight entry per character of the charmap.
=reorder-sections-after= and =reorder-sections-end= are glibc's
spelling of =reorder-section-after= and =reorder-section-end=.

The oracles under =bin/oracle= compare a database with the C library
under =setlocale= for the same locale: every classification and both
case maps of every code point, and the collation of a word list, of
50000 random strings and of each printable character against the next.
The classifications and case maps agree but for 135 code points at the
ends of the CJK extension ranges of =i18n_ctype=: the database
classifies 2B81E-2B81F, 2CEAE-2CEAF, 2EBE1-2EBEF and 3134B-3134F, past
the ends of the ranges, and misses 2EBA1-2EBAF, 2EE1E-2EE2F,
2EE40-2EE5D and 33440-3346F, inside them; the C library follows the
ranges. =C.UTF-8= collates every other code point as the C library
does and ties these. Hypothesis: the expansion of the =<A>..<B>= lines
of the UTF-8 charmap, which come in runs of 64 code points, misplaces
the ends of the runs; open. The collation of =en_US=, =hu_HU=,
=es_ES=, =sv_SE=, =fa_IR=, =ml_IN=, =zh_CN=, =cmn_TW=, =mdf_RU= and
=crh_RU= agrees but for the two classes of the entries below.

Fix: in order. First the rest of the compiler: the tokens' file
names kept alive past =Scanner::close= and the heap corruption found
(the entry below); the transliteration statements written raw, or the
decision that the compiler stops writing what the runtime does not
read; the era records, every byte set, a date that does not parse
reported, the =th_TH= year; the 135 code points, the run-expansion
hypothesis settled against the =<A>..<B>= lines of the UTF-8 charmap.
Then the shared tables together with their charmaps, =i18n=, the
transliteration and collation templates: refreshed leaf definitions
alone leave the shared tables stale. Then the definitions: glibc's
371 files and 233 charmaps (shipped gzipped; the compiler reads plain
files), its =SUPPORTED= in place of =gen_list=
(=etc/config/GNUmakefile.bin= and =tests/src/locale.cpp= read the two
columns of =gen_list= and move =.euro= to =@euro=; the test build
compiles every locale the list names, 502 against 165, so the build
is measured), the legacy names =iw_IL=, =no_NO=, =sr_YU= and
=sr_YU.cyrillic= kept through an explicit mapping. Then the databases
regenerated and the decoded facet values compared with the C library
through =bin/oracle= and with the glibc source baseline, the currency,
numeric and date repairs staged separately. Two decisions precede the
collation entries below: whether a definition without =UNDEFINED=
places the undefined characters as glibc does, and whether the
position-level =IGNORE= mark sorts low.

Check: =localedef= builds every definition in the refreshed
=etc/nls/src= with its charmap, two builds byte-identical. The locale
tests pass against their
pins, re-pinned where the data moved. The decoded currency, numeric
and date values of the 139 shared definitions match the glibc
sources they come from.

** TODO =localedef= corrupts its heap now and then

Four of about 1000 runs of the census over glibc's =SUPPORTED=, ten
at a time, died in the destructor of =Def= (=mg_MG=, =oc_FR=,
=so_DJ=, =kok_IN=): a segmentation fault in the =delete[]= of the
weights of an undefined character whose pointer read 0x1000000000000,
and "double free or corruption" in a =map<string, string>= whose
string held =\377\377\377\377=. Each of the four builds 100 times out
of 100 alone and ten at a time, and memcheck finds no invalid read or
write in them. AddressSanitizer found a use after free: a token keeps
a pointer to the name of the file it came from, and the lists of
=LC_COLLATE= keep tokens past the =Scanner::close= of a copied file,
so a diagnostic prints freed memory (garbled file names in the logs);
that is a read and cannot corrupt the heap by itself. Two more
defects of the kind: the rules word of the element of the undefined
characters (=undef_char_info_=) is never set without =UNDEFINED=, and
=get_installed_locales= (=util/aliases.cpp=) frees with =free= what
=new[]= allocated and names its temporary file with =tmpnam=, which
ten processes at once can share.

Fix: keep the file names alive in the scanner for the tokens; set
the undefined element; then a census under AddressSanitizer with
recovery, ten at a time, to find the write.

Check: two censuses in a row without a signal, and none under
AddressSanitizer.

** TODO Characters outside a collation table sort last, glibc's first

A character that a definition does not name and no =UNDEFINED=
covers gets its own position as its weight at every level
(=add_missing_values= in =util/collate.cpp=), after every defined
character. glibc gives such a character =IGNORE= at the first three
levels and a weight at the fourth: =wcsxfrm= of U+743B, a Han
character outside the pinyin table, writes no weight before the
fourth level under =zh_CN.UTF-8=, so it sorts before every letter.
POSIX leaves the place of an undefined character to the
implementation. The oracles count 1584 disagreements of 50000 random
strings over U+4E00 to U+9FFF in =zh_CN=, all over characters
outside the table; U+061D in =fa_IR=; U+0D00, U+0D04, U+0D3B and
U+0D3C in =ml_IN=; the 9 single-character pairs of 297266 that
differ in =en_US=, at the boundaries of the CJK extension blocks and
over the newest symbols. For these
characters the narrow facet already orders as glibc does and the
wide one does not.

Fix: decide whether a definition without =UNDEFINED= follows glibc;
if it does, give the missing characters =IGNORE= at every level but
the last, and find why the narrow and wide facets part.

Check: the collation oracle over those ranges finds no disagreement
over a character outside the table.

** TODO A character IGNOREd at a level sorts after a weight there

The library and glibc part over strings that differ only in a
character =IGNORE=d at some level. Under =sv_SE=, ü (=IGNORE= at the
fourth level) against ÿ (its code point there), equal at the first
three: glibc puts ü first, the library second. Under =en_US=, "é"
against "é>", where =>= is =IGNORE= at the first three levels: glibc
puts the shorter string first, the library second. The same shape
accounts for the 1 disagreement of 50000 random strings in =en_US=
and 3 in =fa_IR= and 6 in =ml_IN= once the characters outside the
table are left out. Hypothesis: the fourth level of =iso14651_t1= is
=forward,position=, and for an element =IGNORE=d at a position level
the transform writes its =IGNORE= mark, =CHAR_MAX= narrow and
=WCHAR_MAX= wide, which sorts after every weight
(=doc/notes/analysis-collate-transform.md=); the punctuation of the
=<SPECIAL>= section is read backward at the second level, so a
string that mixes it with letters may mix directions as well.

Fix: settle the hypothesis by the transform of each pair against
=wcsxfrm=; then decide where the mark sorts, in =src/collate.cpp=,
or what =localedef= writes.

Check: the collation oracle finds no disagreement over strings of
letters and characters =IGNORE=d at the first three levels in
=en_US=, =sv_SE=, =fa_IR= and =ml_IN=.

* EDG porting

The tree builds with EDG's =eccp= in strict mode (=-A=) as a
conformance check: a question is settled there before a bug goes to
another compiler. The front end is open source as of 2026-09:
github.com/edgcpp/compiler. The first entry is the build; the rest
are what it found. Each error is read against the standard: a library
defect is fixed here, a compiler defect goes to EDG, and the leniency
of GCC or Clang it exposes is recorded. The =WAITING= entries at the
end are defects reported to EDG or glibc.

A first build on 2026-10-06, at 6bb238b6 with EDG 7.0 over gcc 16 and
glibc, took hand changes to the tree. It configured, built the
library, the test driver and the utilities, and built 257 of 272 test
programs. The suite ran without signals or non-zero exits: 10,049,711
assertions, 16 failed. A second build on 2026-10-08, at 48d6dac1 after
the retirement of =export=, with the same hand changes and the same
front end, gave the same table.

** TODO The =eccp= configuration

=etc/config/eccp.config= is back, restored from before the platform
retirement (dd1128a5) and brought current: =-A -x=, =--no_export=
while the =WAITING= =src/punct.cpp= entry stands, the prelinker
(~PRELINKFLAGS = --prelink_objects~, the step before =ar= in
=GNUmakefile.lib= and =GNUmakefile.rwt=, =*.ti= in the clean rules),
=-lpthread=, and =etc/config/eccp_builtins.h= on =--preinclude_macros=.
The =__EDG__= guards went with the platform retirement (d743fa8d,
12475dfe); comments that name =eccp= survive in =_iosbase.h=,
=_traits.h= and five characterizations, and so do the =eccp= rows of
=etc/config/xfail.txt=.

The default dialect of =eccp= is C++03 (=__cplusplus= is =199711L=).
No dialect option is needed. The C headers are a property of the
=eccp= installation, not of the tree: it searches the resident gcc's
freestanding headers and glibc's, and its macro table carries the
target facts gcc's =float.h= and =limits.h= read.

gcc's =stddef.h= spells =offsetof= as =__builtin_offsetof=, a keyword
EDG enters only when it emulates GNU, Microsoft or Clang. The name is
reserved, so strict mode may provide it; reported as
https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/185. =eccp_builtins.h=
maps it onto =__INTADDR__=, EDG's own =offsetof= primitive; the
characterization "a working offsetof() in <stddef.h>" passes with it.
A =-D= cannot carry the definition: =libc_headers.sh= expands =$CPPFLAGS=
unquoted, the shell splits it at its spaces, and every header probe
fails. The file leaves when EDG enters the keyword.

With no feature macro on the command line, =make config= under
=eccp -A= (11S) configures (what differs from gcc's =config.h= is the
entry "The configuration under =eccp= against GCC's"), and
=make lib= then compiles all 98 sources: the
C99 functions the library calls fall back on C90 behind their
characterizations, and the library's own C header facts moved from
=_config-gcc.h= to =_config.h= so that every compiler takes the
wrapper model (the entry "The pure C headers"). The test driver, the
utilities and the tests do not build yet (the entry "The test driver,
the utilities and the tests under =eccp -A=").

Fix: the entries below, in order. The gcc configuration is unchanged:
its =makefile.in= gains an empty =PRELINKFLAGS= and =make -n lib=
shows no prelink step.

Check: an =eccp= build directory configures, builds the library, the
utilities and the tests, and runs the suite. Its table is pinned
beside the six.

** TODO Run-time differences from GCC

Under =eccp -A=:
- =26.valarray.transcend=: nine failed assertions on the =float=
  functions (=std::acos (valarray<float>)= and kin) in the first
  build, where =<cmath>= forwarded its =float= and =long double=
  overloads to the C99 functions glibc withholds from strict C++03.
  The overloads forward to the =double= functions under
  =_RWSTD_NO_C99_MATH= now; the row is to be rerun.
- =18.limits.traps=: =numeric_limits<bool>::traps= is true, as for
  every integer type, but the test's =bool= division by zero did not
  trap. Outside the test, =bool= division by zero traps under =eccp=
  too. The same row fails in an optimized GCC build (12D, 13
  assertions) and once in 12D with Clang: the test counts on undefined
  behavior surviving the optimizer, which is the first suspect.
- =18.support.dynamic=: =bad_alloc::what ()= returns a bad string.
  The first question is whose =bad_alloc= runs, the library's or that
  of EDG's runtime.
- =22.locale.num.put= runs 1,399 assertions against 1,485.

Fix: read each against the standard and the characterization that
decides it.

Check: the rows pass, or the reason is recorded.

** TODO The configuration under =eccp= against GCC's

=config.h= under =eccp -A= differs from GCC's 11S beyond the other
entries of this chapter: no atomic built-ins, no =_RWSTD_THREAD= and
no TLS; =STD_TYPE_INFO= fails (the characterization declares its own
=type_info=; 12475dfe removed the =#pragma define_type_info= that
served =eccp=); =TYPE_INFO_DTOR= and =LIBC_EXCEPTION_SPEC= (#536,
an exception specification incompatible with glibc's) fail; the
special-member characterizations of the exception classes differ.
=STD_NOTHROW= fails to link: =libC.a= defines no =std::nothrow=
object. =long long= and extern templates are absent, which is
correct for C++03.

Without a feature macro glibc shows strict C++03 the ISO names only,
and the POSIX and XSI characterizations fail on an undefined
identifier: =DAYLIGHT=, =TIMEZONE=, =TM_GMTOFF=, =MADVISE=
(=caddr_t=), =THREAD_SAFE_EXCEPTIONS= (=pthread_setconcurrency=),
=VSNPRINTF_RETURN= (=vsnprintf=), =VA_COPY=, =LCONV_INT_FMAT=. The
first build of 2026-10-06 did not see them because its command line
pinned =_XOPEN_SOURCE=. The library does not ask for these names: it
builds without them. Whether the test driver, the utilities and the
tests state a feature level is the entry "The test driver, the
utilities and the tests under =eccp -A=".

Fix: read each difference against the standard and the
characterization.

Check: every difference is correct for C++03 or has an entry.

** TODO The test driver, the utilities and the tests under =eccp -A=

The library builds under =eccp -A= with no feature macro on the
command line. The test driver, the utilities and the tests do not:
without a feature macro glibc shows strict C++03 the ISO names only,
and these programs are POSIX programs, and glibc withholds the C95
wide I/O functions too, which the library's =<cwchar>= re-exports.
The first build of 2026-10-06 did not see this because its command
line pinned =_XOPEN_SOURCE=.

- =tests/src/thread.cpp= (=popen=, =pclose=),
  =tests/src/environ.cpp= (=unsetenv=), =tests/src/process.cpp=
  (=include/ansi/signal.h= under =_RWSTD_NO_DEPRECATED_C_HEADERS=
  stops at line 63), =util/path.cpp= (=symlink=), =util/memchk.cpp=
  (=getpagesize=, =mkstemp=), =util/time.cpp= (=std::sscanf=
  undeclared, cause not read).
- Fifteen test programs stop in =include/ansi/cwchar=, lines
  143-157: =using ::fwprintf;= and the other wide I/O functions of
  C95, which glibc declares under =__USE_ISOC95= only. C++03's C
  library is C90 with Amendment 1, so the names are the library's
  to provide; glibc exports them. Dropping them from =std= is not
  conforming; declaring them is right while the symbols exist.
- The programs the first build could not compile either (the entries
  below) are unchanged.

Fix: undecided. The POSIX sources can state their feature level, as
=tests/src/file.cpp= does; the C95 names want either a
characterization that lets =<cwchar>= declare them itself or a
decision that glibc's gap is glibc's.

Check: the driver, the utilities and the tests build under =eccp -A=
with no feature macro on the command line, the suite runs and its
table is pinned, and the GCC and Clang tables do not move.

** TODO The pure C headers

=include/ansi/_c*.h=, RW's own C90 declarations inside =std=, are the
model a strict compiler would want: exactly the C++03 C library,
whatever glibc hides. They are selected by =_RWSTD_USE_PURE_C_HEADERS=
and no configuration has compiled them since the platform retirement.
Two defects stand in the way of a strict build with them:
- =_cstdio.h= declares =vfprintf=, =vprintf= and =vsprintf= with
  =_RWSTD_VA_LIST=, the layout-compatible =__rw_va_list= of
  =VA_LIST.cpp=, and =_cstdarg.h= defines =va_start= and =va_arg= by
  byte arithmetic. On an ABI whose =va_list= is a structure (x86_64,
  aarch64) the compiler's =va_list= converts to neither, so a call
  with a real =va_list= does not compile and the macros compute the
  wrong thing. =eccp= resolves =<stdarg.h>= to its own header first,
  so the library sees the compiler's =va_list=.
- =_cstdlib.h:141= qualifies =qsort= with =_RWSTD_C=, which is empty
  under =_RWSTD_NO_LIBC_IN_STD=; the pure model has no global
  =qsort= at that point. =bsearch= ten lines above calls its C
  function unqualified. Since the library facts moved to =_config.h=
  the macro is undefined for every compiler and the line compiles;
  the inconsistency stays.

Fix: a decision. The pure headers either defer to the compiler's
=<stdarg.h>= for =va_list= and the macros, which makes =<cstdarg>=
and the v-functions of =<cstdio>= wrappers, or they retire.

Check: a build with =_RWSTD_USE_PURE_C_HEADERS= under =eccp -A= and
under gcc, or the headers gone.

** TODO The C++ overloads of the wide string functions under =eccp=

glibc declares the C++ overloads of =wcschr=, =wcspbrk=, =wcsrchr=,
=wcsstr= and =wmemchr= behind =__CORRECT_ISO_CPP_WCHAR_H_PROTO=, with
=__asm= labels strict mode rejects. =CORRECT_ISO_CPP_WCHAR_H_PROTO=
characterizes whether the compiler accepts them, and =<cwchar>= and
=<wchar.h>= define the macro only when it does. Under =eccp -A= glibc
then declares the C prototypes, =<cwchar>= re-exports them, and
=21.cwchar= fails five assertions on the const overloads, as it did
under Clang before 5569cc60.

Fix: give a compiler without the glibc overloads the const-correct
pair in =std=, the way the Clang repair did.

Check: =21.cwchar= passes under =eccp -A= and its GCC and Clang rows
do not move.

** TODO Tests that GCC and Clang let through

Ten test programs do not compile under =eccp -A= for causes in the
test:
- =typeid= without =<typeinfo>=, ill-formed (5.2.8p6):
  =tests/support/18.exception.cpp:883=,
  =tests/support/18.support.rtti.cpp:99=.
- =std::size_t= without =<cstddef>=: =tests/self/0.ctype.cpp:51=,
  =tests/localization/22.locale.facet.mt.cpp:130=.
- No newline at the end of the file:
  =tests/numerics/26.gslice.array.cassign.cpp=.
- =long long= unguarded: =tests/iostream/27.istream.fmat.arith.cpp:1028=.
- POSIX names without a feature macro: =getcwd= in
  =tests/localization/22.locale.messages.cpp:616=, =BC_BASE_MAX= in
  =tests/regress/18.c.limits.stdcxx-988.cpp:53=.
- A UTF-8 =à= in a narrow string literal, which =eccp= rejects as not
  representable (#3439); the mapping is implementation-defined:
  =tests/regress/22.locale.collate.stdcxx-1010.cpp:71=.
- Template instantiation 435 deep:
  =tests/localization/22.locale.id.mt.cpp:98=. C++03 Annex B
  recommends at least 17.

Fix: repair each test; the instantiation depth is a decision of its
own.

Check: each compiles under =eccp -A=, and the GCC and Clang tables do
not move.

** TODO Calls EDG matches to no function

Four test programs fail with error #304, no instance of a function
template or overloaded function matches the argument list. Each call
is dependent and each callee it means has internal linkage.
- =tests/iostream/27.std.manip.cpp:518= (=test<charT, Traits>=),
  =tests/iostream/27.filebuf.cpp:2019= (=test_ctors<charT>=),
  =tests/localization/22.locale.codecvt.cpp:680=
  (=test_libstd_do_in<CodeCvtT>=): calls by template-id to =static=
  function templates. Six lines reproduce it: a dependent call to a
  =static= function template, by template-id or deduced, fails under
  =eccp -A= and compiles under =eccp --c++11 -A=, =-x= and GCC's
  =-std=c++98=. C++03 14.6.4.2 finds only functions with external
  linkage for a dependent call by unqualified-id; C++11 dropped the
  restriction. Whether the C++03 rule covers a template-id is to be
  read in the C++03 text; if it does not, the rejection is an EDG
  defect.
- =tests/localization/22.locale.collate.cpp:1159=: an unqualified call
  =check_NUL_collate (charTname, locname, charT ())=. EDG considers
  only the six-argument template; the =static= non-template overloads
  the call means (=:1072=, =:1108=) are not candidates. Small programs
  of the same shape compile under =eccp -A=, so the cause is not yet
  known; C-Vise is the next step.

The characterization =STATICS_IN_TEMPLATE= fails the same way, #304
on =static_function=, so =config.h= under =eccp -A= carries
=_RWSTD_NO_STATICS_IN_TEMPLATE=. =src/num_get.cpp= drew #304 three
times on =__rw::__rw_validate= while the =_RWSTD_FLT_*= macros were
missing; with =FLOAT= passing it compiles, so those were a cascade.

Fix: read the C++03 text; then either give the callees external
linkage or report to EDG.

Check: each compiles under =eccp -A=, the characterization passes,
and the GCC and Clang tables do not move.

** WAITING =src/punct.cpp= under the export model

The library uses neither =export= nor implicit inclusion since
11b144b9..48d6dac1. =eccp -A= still turns its export model on, and
under it EDG fails an internal assertion in =src/punct.cpp= at line
113 (#2656, in =find_local_static_variable_init=). The other 97
library sources compile. The 136 errors of the first build, 98 of them
"already been defined" in the =.cc= files, came from =_EXPORT= and are
gone. Reported as https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/233,
with a one-line candidate patch: a block scope that lowering adds for
a return-value temporary has no parent.

Fix: EDG's. Until then =eccp.config= passes =--no_export=.

Check: the =eccp= build needs no =--no_export= with a front end that
fixes it.

** WAITING =_setjmp= in the C that EDG generates for =25.libc=

=eccp= compiles C++ to C and gcc compiles the C. For
=tests/algorithms/25.libc.cpp= the generated C declares =_setjmp= as
=int (long *)=; glibc's =<setjmp.h>= declares it
=int (struct __jmp_buf_tag *)=. gcc rejects the conflict at
=25.libc.cpp:118=, and the test does not build under =eccp -A=.

A defect of the front end's C back end, in every mode: a translation
unit that calls =setjmp= and has a =try= block. The =try= lowering
declares its own =_setjmp= and does not look for the program's.
glibc spells =setjmp (env)= as =_setjmp (env)=. Reported as
https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/234.

Fix: EDG's. The test and the library need no change.

Check: =25.libc= builds under =eccp -A= with a front end that fixes
it.

** WAITING Names the prelinker cannot decode

The prelinker reports "an error occurred during name decoding" 22
times while it builds the library under =eccp -A=. c++filt decodes
the same names. All 22 have one shape, e.g.
=_ZN4__rw12__rw_extractIcSt11char_traitsIcEtEERSt13basic_istreamIT_T0_ES7_RT1_=,
=__rw::__rw_extract<char, std::char_traits<char>, unsigned short>=.
The test programs draw it for about 2,100 more.

A defect of EDG's tools, not of these names: on Linux =edg_decode=
decodes no IA-64 mangled name at all, and the prelinker warns for
every such name that contains =__=. A two-line program with a
function template in a namespace =__n= reproduces it. The prelinker
uses decoded names only in its messages; template instances are
assigned and the library builds. Reported as
https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/240.

Fix: EDG's. The library needs no change.

Check: a build with a front end that fixes it shows no decoding
warnings.

** WAITING =27.ostream=: string literals defined twice in the C that EDG generates

=tests/iostream/27.ostream.cpp= does not build under =eccp -A=. gcc
rejects the generated C: it defines the string literals of
=test_formatted<charT>= twice (=_ZZ14test_formattedIcEvT_Es_0= and
others, for =char= and =wchar_t=). The failure shows in the
prelinker's recompile only because that is where =test_formatted= is
instantiated.

A defect of the front end in strict mode, in every dialect: a string
literal of an inline function or a template instance is defined once
for each C-style cast of it to =const signed char*= or
=const unsigned char*= passed as a function argument, and every
definition has the same name. The =SCH= and =UCH= macros of the test
make such casts. Reported as
https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/243.

Fix: EDG's. The test needs no change.

Check: =27.ostream= builds under =eccp -A= with a front end that fixes
it.

** WAITING glibc's =signbit= assumes a GNU built-in in C++

glibc's =<math.h>= gives a C++ compiler that is neither GCC 6+ nor
Clang 3.3+ ~#define signbit(x) __builtin_signbitl (x)~. A compiler
that does not emulate GCC has no such built-in: under =eccp= with the
C99 names requested, =src/fpclass.h= fails. A regression in glibc
2.27, from the fix for its bug 22296. Reported as
https://sourceware.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=34717.

Without the C99 names, as in C++03 with no feature macro, glibc
defines no =fpclassify= and =src/fpclass.h= takes its stub branch:
=__rw_signbit=, =__rw_isnan= and =__rw_isinf= return false,
=__rw_isfinite= true. That is the =eccp -A= build today: the library
asks glibc for no C99 name. How =num_put= classifies floating values
there shows in the run table, once the tests build.

Fix: glibc's, for the header.

Check: =src/fpclass.h= compiles under =eccp -A= with the C99 names
requested and a glibc that fixes it.

* Documentation

Documents that describe the library as it is, for a reader, not for
the record. They go under =doc/notes= with the =ref-= prefix. Each
entry names the document, what it must answer and where the material
is. They are prose: no check beyond a reading. Roughly in order of
payback.

** TODO README section 5 and the configuration

The original README says to set =CPPOPTS= and =CXXOPTS= on the make
command line to change compiler options. The configuration does not
see them, by design. =config.h= is a function of the compiler, its
version and the build type; what the configuration must see goes in
the =CONFIG= file. A dialect option given only on the command line
configures one language and builds the library in another.

Fix: prose, no patch. The per-platform manual step the README
describes is a defect to move away from. =README.md= says so; the
original README stays as it is.

Check: none; this is prose.

** TODO A tour of the locale subsystem

